//! City Action: the v2 prototype of the city action RPG (not a shipping game yet).
//!
//! It proves the feel the design memo (`action-game-v2-design.md` in the workspace)
//! asks for, on shared parts only:
//!
//! - A skinned hero (a KayKit knight as the test double) driven by standard
//!   motion names (`attack1`..`attack3`, `shoot`, `hit`, `die`) through
//!   [`crate::game_core::anim`], so the model can be swapped without game code
//!   changing. Combat timing comes from the same numbers the clips are sped to.
//! - Manual attacks on kit-drawn buttons: a sword combo and a
//!   charged magic bolt (hold MAGIC to charge, release to fire — the longer
//!   the hold, the bigger and stronger the bolt), all fed through
//!   [`stick::ActionButtons`], whose tap
//!   latch keeps sub-frame taps and whose buffer reserves an early next-stage
//!   press. Damage, knockback, hitstun, projectiles and the charge tiers live
//!   in [`crate::game_core::combat`]: weapon volumes and hurt cylinders stay
//!   split.
//! - Four spells on one charge ladder shape (v3): neutral, fire (a burn
//!   that ticks), ice (a slow) and thunder (a chain past the burst),
//!   switched by the small chip by the MAGIC button or `Q`. combat owns
//!   the effect mechanics; this kit owns the four ladders' numbers.
//! - One sword on one combo shape: the standard sword, a
//!   [`combat::Weapon`] — six [`combat::ComboStage`]s and a tempo.
//!   (v3 shipped six switchable swords — standard, short, broadsword,
//!   ember, frost, volt — swapped by a HUD chip, dropped and re-taken
//!   as items. The variation system was removed on Saka's call,
//!   2026-11-15: its swap machinery re-dressed the hero's skinned GLB
//!   on every exchange, and three fix rounds never made the dropped
//!   sword behave on a real phone. One sword, no swaps, no weapon
//!   items; the elemental *spells* stay, all four owned from the
//!   start — their rune-unlock gate went the same way on Saka's
//!   call, 2026-11-16: a rune was takeable, but the detour never
//!   paid for itself.)
//! - The combo is a tree (Saka's call, 2026-11-14 for the first
//!   branch; generalized 2026-11-16 at his direction: the Musou
//!   shape as an engine part; extended 2026-11-18 by the motion
//!   round and simplified 2026-11-19 on his device check: MELEE
//!   is one button again, after a day split in two). The chain
//!   alternates its clip families by depth — wide on the even
//!   depths, diagonal on the odd — so a mash never repeats one
//!   swing; six cuts walk to the **spin finisher** (one full
//!   turn; the ult keeps three), and a mash that presses through
//!   the spin's window fires the **big shot** forward as the
//!   chain's finale. A MAGIC press inside any swing's
//!   reservation window branches at that stage — every stage
//!   has one: combat's [`ComboChain`] owns the input rules, this
//!   kit declares the moves on a [`ComboTree`]: cut 1 branches
//!   to a lunging wide **sweep** (the sword's own second stage,
//!   widened), cuts 2-4 to a point-blank **burst** of the
//!   selected spell at tier 1, and cuts 5-6 and the spin to the
//!   **big shot** — the selected spell thrown forward as one
//!   huge bolt with a two-handed cast. Outside a window MAGIC
//!   is the charged bolt, as always — a press that lands before
//!   the window opens is swept unfired, never a branch.
//! - A crowd of **non-skinned** zombies (pose-baked GLBs as instanced static
//!   props, swapped by state; single-GLB and capsule fallbacks), 30 / 45 / 60
//!   switchable, and circle-vs-AABB building push-out shared by the hero, the
//!   crowd and knockback slides.
//!
//! - XP and growth (the survivor port): every kill leaves an XP gem;
//!   banking gems climbs Dusk Horde's curve (`5 + (Lv-1) x 4`) and a
//!   level freezes the world on a three-card pick — seven passives
//!   that move real stats here (damage, max HP, speed, magnet, gauge,
//!   charge, dodge), five levels each, plus Moonlit's level-up heal.
//!
//! - Stages and saves (v5): three stages over the one Harlem build —
//!   the same streets re-lit and re-tuned per stage (tier pace, body
//!   counts, HP, boss order and triggers, fog, lamps, the street
//!   track). The stage's last boss ends the run: STAGE CLEAR, the
//!   next stage unlocks, and [`game_core::progress::Progress`] keeps
//!   the unlocks and per-stage best scores on the device through
//!   `save_json` (the manifest's `page.save`). Once a second stage is
//!   reached, a run opens on a stage-select beat — the field idles
//!   behind three cards until one is tapped.

